lanar graph can be colored with 4\nor less colors.\n1.3. Inductively, a finite simple graph G is contractible if either G = K1 or if\nthere is a vertex x in G such that both S(x) and G −x are contractible. A graph\nis a d-sphere if it is either the empty graph 0 (a −1-sphere) or if it is a graph for\nwhich all unit spheres S(x) are (d −1)-spheres and such that removing any of its\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShiva Soleimany Dizicheh - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/shiva-soleimany-dizicheh)\nciteturn8search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## Papers by Shiva Soleimany Dizicheh ... In this paper, we give a constructive approach to study the acyclic matchings in cyclic groups.\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n[Input: Search by title, author, BibTeX key, or arXiv URL...] [Button: Search]\n\n## Papers by Shiva Soleimany Dizicheh\n\n1 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\n\n2018-08-15 v3\n\nMohsen Aliabadi, Shiva Soleimany Dizicheh\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA note on matchings in abelian groups\n\nThe question of finding sets of monomials which are removable from a generic homogeneous polynomial through a linear change in its variables was raised by E. K. Wakeford in 1916. This linear algebra question motivated J. Losonczy to define the concept of acyclic matchings in Z n, and later in abelian groups. In this paper, we give a constructive approach to study the acyclic matchings in cyclic groups. We also introduce the notion of weakly matched subsets and investigate its relation with matchings in abelian groups.\n\ncyclic group group matching polynomial\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJava Based Distributed Learning Platform (https://www.davidpublisher.com/Public/uploads/Contribute/5b977e193bc0e.pdf)\nciteturn8search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 11 months 